      "state_doh_release": {
        "n_unique": 242,
        "top_values": {
          "Alaska Department of Health Pilots Drug Checking and Fentanyl Test Strip Distribution\nBeginning this month the Department will support a pilot program providing fentanyl test strips and xylazine immunoassay strips through approximately 29 authorized harm reduction sites across Alaska. Drug checking allows individuals who use drugs to detect adulterants prior to use, an evidence-based intervention shown to reduce overdose risk. Test strips are legal under Alaska statute when used for harm reduction and are distributed at no cost to the recipient. Site locations are listed on the Department's harm reduction webpage. --- Alaska Department of Health Announces Expanded Over-the-Counter Naloxone Availability\nFollowing the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approval of over-the-counter naloxone nasal spray, Alaska Department of Health confirms that Narcan is now available without a prescription at participating retail pharmacies statewide, including major chains. The Department continues to operate 149 community distribution sites and supplies free naloxone to approximately 69 community-based organizations through state and federal opioid response funding. Residents may also request free naloxone by mail through the state harm-reduction program. The Department reminds the public that naloxone reverses opioid overdose only and is safe to administer; bystanders cannot harm a person who does not have opioids in their system.": 1,
          "Idaho Department of Health and Welfare Issues Public Health Advisory on Cluster of Suspected Fentanyl-Involved Overdoses\nIdaho health officials are alerting clinicians, first responders, and the public to a recent cluster of 15 suspected nonfatal overdose events reported in the Meridian area. Preliminary toxicology indicates involvement of illicitly manufactured fentanyl, with several specimens also positive for xylazine. Naloxone remains the recommended reversal agent; clinicians should be prepared to administer repeat doses given fentanyl potency. Community members are encouraged to obtain naloxone through participating pharmacies or county health departments at no cost. Anyone witnessing an overdose should call 911; the Idaho Good Samaritan law provides limited protection from prosecution for those seeking help. --- Idaho Department of Health and Welfare Announces Expanded Over-the-Counter Naloxone Availability\nFollowing the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approval of over-the-counter naloxone nasal spray, Idaho Department of Health and Welfare confirms that Narcan is now available without a prescription at participating retail pharmacies statewide, including major chains. The Department continues to operate 42 community distribution sites and supplies free naloxone to approximately 23 community-based organizations through state and federal opioid response funding. Residents may also request free naloxone by mail through the state harm-reduction program. The Department reminds the public that naloxone reverses opioid overdose only and is safe to administer; bystanders cannot harm a person who does not have opioids in their system.": 1,
          "Massachusetts Department of Public Health Issues Alert on Detection of Nitazene Synthetic Opioids\nState public health laboratory results have detected isotonitazene, N-pyrrolidino-protonitazene, and related synthetic opioids in samples submitted from 5 jurisdictions in the past 30 days. Nitazene analogues are extremely potent \u2014 some exceeding fentanyl in laboratory potency assays \u2014 and may not be reliably detected by point-of-care fentanyl test strips alone. Naloxone remains the recommended reversal agent; multiple sequential doses may be required. Clinicians treating suspected opioid overdose should consider repeat naloxone administration, airway management, and prolonged observation. --- Massachusetts Department of Public Health Opens Naloxone Vending Machines and Harm-Reduction Kiosks\nNaloxone vending machines providing free Narcan nasal spray, fentanyl test strips, and overdose education materials are now operational at 11 locations across Massachusetts, including Worcester, Boston, Springfield. The machines operate 24 hours a day and require no identification, prescription, or fee. Use of the machines is anonymous and protected under state public health authority. The Department will continue to expand kiosk placement in partnership with libraries, transit hubs, and community organizations.": 1,
          "Maryland Department of Health Launches Quick Response Teams in Additional Counties\nQuick Response Teams (QRTs) consisting of behavioral health peers, paramedics, and law enforcement liaisons will begin operations in Davidson, Marion, Baltimore, Allegheny counties. QRTs follow up with overdose survivors within 72 hours of an emergency response, offering naloxone, fentanyl test strips where authorized, and warm handoffs to medication for opioid use disorder treatment. Funding for the expansion comes from a $8,400,000 CDC Overdose Data to Action grant administered by the Department's behavioral health division.": 1,
          "Maine Department of Health and Human Services Expands Access to Medication for Opioid Use Disorder\nThe Department announces the addition of approximately 245 new buprenorphine-prescribing clinicians to the Maine treatment network as a result of the federal removal of the X-waiver requirement and continued state-led training programs. Buprenorphine, methadone, and extended-release naltrexone remain the first-line treatments for opioid use disorder. Residents seeking medication for opioid use disorder may use the SAMHSA treatment locator at FindTreatment.gov or contact the state addiction services helpline. Medicaid covers all three medications and counseling services without prior authorization. --- Maine Department of Health and Human Services Launches Quick Response Teams in Additional Counties\nQuick Response Teams (QRTs) consisting of behavioral health peers, paramedics, and law enforcement liaisons will begin operations in Fulton, Philadelphia, Baltimore, Mecklenburg counties. QRTs follow up with overdose survivors within 72 hours of an emergency response, offering naloxone, fentanyl test strips where authorized, and warm handoffs to medication for opioid use disorder treatment. Funding for the expansion comes from a $7,100,000 State Opioid Response grant administered by the Department's behavioral health division. --- Maine Department of Health and Human Services Announces Expanded Over-the-Counter Naloxone Availability\nFollowing the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approval of over-the-counter naloxone nasal spray, Maine Department of Health and Human Services confirms that Narcan is now available without a prescription at participating retail pharmacies statewide, including major chains. The Department continues to operate 132 community distribution sites and supplies free naloxone to approximately 31 community-based organizations through state and federal opioid response funding. Residents may also request free naloxone by mail through the state harm-reduction program. The Department reminds the public that naloxone reverses opioid overdose only and is safe to administer; bystanders cannot harm a person who does not have opioids in their system.": 1,
          "Minnesota Department of Health Warns of Xylazine Mixed Into Illicit Drug Supply\nToxicology reports from Minnesota medical examiners and law-enforcement seizures continue to show xylazine, a non-opioid veterinary sedative, mixed into the illicit fentanyl supply. Xylazine \u2014 also known as 'tranq' \u2014 was detected in approximately 50 percent of fentanyl-positive specimens analyzed in the past quarter. Xylazine causes prolonged sedation and can produce severe soft-tissue wounds. Naloxone reverses the opioid component of these mixtures and should still be administered when overdose is suspected. Individuals presenting with non-healing wounds should seek medical evaluation and be screened for substance use treatment options including buprenorphine and methadone. --- Minnesota Department of Health Issues Alert on Detection of Nitazene Synthetic Opioids\nState public health laboratory results have detected isotonitazene, N-pyrrolidino-protonitazene, and related synthetic opioids in samples submitted from 4 jurisdictions in the past 30 days. Nitazene analogues are extremely potent \u2014 some exceeding fentanyl in laboratory potency assays \u2014 and may not be reliably detected by point-of-care fentanyl test strips alone. Naloxone remains the recommended reversal agent; multiple sequential doses may be required. Clinicians treating suspected opioid overdose should consider repeat naloxone administration, airway management, and prolonged observation.": 1,
          "Missouri Department of Health and Senior Services Launches Quick Response Teams in Additional Counties\nQuick Response Teams (QRTs) consisting of behavioral health peers, paramedics, and law enforcement liaisons will begin operations in Kanawha, Cuyahoga, Baltimore counties. QRTs follow up with overdose survivors within 72 hours of an emergency response, offering naloxone, fentanyl test strips where authorized, and warm handoffs to medication for opioid use disorder treatment. Funding for the expansion comes from a $3,100,000 State Opioid Response grant administered by the Department's behavioral health division.": 1,
          "Mississippi State Department of Health Launches Quick Response Teams in Additional Counties\nQuick Response Teams (QRTs) consisting of behavioral health peers, paramedics, and law enforcement liaisons will begin operations in Fulton, Cook, Marion, Suffolk counties. QRTs follow up with overdose survivors within 72 hours of an emergency response, offering naloxone, fentanyl test strips where authorized, and warm handoffs to medication for opioid use disorder treatment. Funding for the expansion comes from a $3,900,000 HRSA grant administered by the Department's behavioral health division. --- Mississippi State Department of Health Authorizes Expanded Syringe Services Program Coverage\nThe Department has approved expansion of authorized syringe services programs to additional counties under Mississippi public health authority. Syringe services programs reduce transmission of HIV and hepatitis C, connect individuals to medication for opioid use disorder, and distribute naloxone and fentanyl test strips. Decades of peer-reviewed evidence and CDC guidance support syringe services programs as a core component of the public health response to drug-related harms.": 1,
          "Montana Department of Public Health and Human Services Expands Access to Medication for Opioid Use Disorder\nThe Department announces the addition of approximately 79 new buprenorphine-prescribing clinicians to the Montana treatment network as a result of the federal removal of the X-waiver requirement and continued state-led training programs. Buprenorphine, methadone, and extended-release naltrexone remain the first-line treatments for opioid use disorder. Residents seeking medication for opioid use disorder may use the SAMHSA treatment locator at FindTreatment.gov or contact the state addiction services helpline. Medicaid covers all three medications and counseling services without prior authorization.": 1,
          "New Hampshire Department of Health and Human Services Warns of Xylazine Mixed Into Illicit Drug Supply\nToxicology reports from New Hampshire medical examiners and law-enforcement seizures continue to show xylazine, a non-opioid veterinary sedative, mixed into the illicit fentanyl supply. Xylazine \u2014 also known as 'tranq' \u2014 was detected in approximately 42 percent of fentanyl-positive specimens analyzed in the past quarter. Xylazine causes prolonged sedation and can produce severe soft-tissue wounds. Naloxone reverses the opioid component of these mixtures and should still be administered when overdose is suspected. Individuals presenting with non-healing wounds should seek medical evaluation and be screened for substance use treatment options including buprenorphine and methadone. --- New Hampshire Department of Health and Human Services Launches Quick Response Teams in Additional Counties\nQuick Response Teams (QRTs) consisting of behavioral health peers, paramedics, and law enforcement liaisons will begin operations in Davidson, Cabell, Cuyahoga, Jefferson counties. QRTs follow up with overdose survivors within 72 hours of an emergency response, offering naloxone, fentanyl test strips where authorized, and warm handoffs to medication for opioid use disorder treatment. Funding for the expansion comes from a $7,000,000 SAMHSA grant administered by the Department's behavioral health division.": 1
        }
